CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a three-dimensional coordinate diagram assembled from three translucent coordinate planes that cut space apart. The vertical axis is labeled z, the axis running to the right is labeled y, and the axis coming forward toward the lower left is labeled x. A pale teal horizontal sheet represents the xy-plane, a peach-colored vertical sheet stretches across the width of the picture, and a blue-violet vertical sheet crosses it at a right angle, all three meeting at the origin. Text set into the wedges between the planes gives the sign pattern of the coordinates there: the near upper wedge carries a bold roman numeral I above the triple (+, +, +), and the surrounding regions are marked (-, -, +), (+, -, +), (-, +, +), (-, +, -), (+, -, -), and (+, +, -), the eighth region being hidden behind the sheets. Portions of the axes drawn as dashed lines indicate where they pass behind a plane. Labeling each region by its signs lets the reader read off directly which side of every coordinate plane that region lies on. The picture carries the familiar division of the plane into four quadrants over into the division of space into eight octants.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - translucent pale teal horizontal square sheet = xy-plane (z = 0)
  - translucent peach vertical square sheet spanning left-right = yz-plane (x = 0)
  - translucent blue-violet vertical square sheet receding into depth = xz-plane (y = 0)
  - z-axis: vertical line through origin with arrowhead at top, italic bold label 'z'
  - y-axis: line to screen-right with arrowhead, italic bold label 'y'
  - x-axis: line coming forward toward lower-left with arrowhead, italic bold label 'x'
  - dashed rendering of the axis segments that pass behind a plane (negative x segment running back-right, negative y segment behind the blue sheet, negative z segment below the teal sheet)
  - origin point where all three planes and axes meet
  - bold roman numeral 'I' set in the near upper wedge
  - octant sign-triple text labels: (+, +, +), (-, -, +), (+, -, +), (-, +, +), (-, +, -), (+, -, -), (+, +, -), and the hidden (-, -, -)
  - thin outline strokes along the free edges of each translucent sheet

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World scale: each coordinate plane is a square sheet spanning -3 to +3 in its two in-plane directions (6 x 6 units), all centered on the origin, so the three sheets mutually bisect each other. Axes are drawn from -3.4 to +3.6 with cone arrowheads (length ~0.3, radius ~0.1) only on the positive ends: +Z straight up, +Y to screen-right, +X forward toward the viewer's lower-left. Axis labels as bold italic sprites: 'z' just left of the top arrowhead, 'y' just below/right of the +y arrowhead, 'x' just below the +x arrowhead. Plane identity and color (all ~0.35-0.45 opacity, double-sided, no depth-write so all eight wedges stay legible): xy-plane (z=0) pale teal #a9d3ca with darker teal edge #4f9b8e; xz-plane (y=0) blue-violet #9a9bc8 with edge #5a5b96; yz-plane (x=0) peach #f7c19a with edge #e08a52. The peach yz-sheet is the one that reads as a wide front-facing rectangle spanning the full picture width; the blue-violet xz-sheet crosses it perpendicularly and recedes along the x direction; the teal xy-sheet lies flat. Sign-triple labels are billboarded dark-gray (#333) text sprites placed at the centroid of each octant, at (±1.6, ±1.6, ±1.6) in (x, y, z) world coords, which reproduces the source layout: (+,+,+) at (1.6,1.6,1.6) upper center-right with a bold 'I' sprite 0.5 units above it; (-,-,+) at (-1.6,-1.6,1.6) top of frame; (+,-,+) at (1.6,-1.6,1.6) upper left; (-,+,+) at (-1.6,1.6,1.6) upper right; (+,-,-) at (1.6,-1.6,-1.6) lower left; (+,+,-) at (1.6,1.6,-1.6) bottom center; (-,+,-) at (-1.6,1.6,-1.6) lower right; (-,-,-) at (-1.6,-1.6,-1.6) is the hidden eighth region (render dimmed/occluded until the scene is orbited). Axis segments that pass behind a sheet are drawn dashed in black (dash 0.12, gap 0.1); solid black elsewhere, line width ~2. Hover pick regions are eight invisible 3 x 3 x 3 boxes occupying the corner cubes of the [-3,3]^3 block; the highlighted wedge fills with a 0.12-opacity tint of the source palette and its label scales up ~1.6x.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "that the eight wedges are genuine 3D regions of space — rotating reveals the octant hidden behind the sheets and shows how each wedge is bounded by three coordinate planes"
  },
  {
    "id": "hoverOctant",
    "type": "hover",
    "teaches": "that a point's three coordinate signs are read off directly from which side of each coordinate plane its wedge lies on; the hovered wedge brightens and its (±, ±, ±) triple is enlarged beside the axes"
  },
  {
    "id": "planeEmphasis",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "Emphasize plane",
    "options": [
      "none",
      "xy-plane (z = 0)",
      "xz-plane (y = 0)",
      "yz-plane (x = 0)"
    ],
    "default": "none",
    "teaches": "that crossing one coordinate plane flips exactly one coordinate sign — the chosen plane opaques and the two half-spaces on either side of it recolor into a sign-positive and sign-negative pair"
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "The three coordinate planes",
    "narration": "Three translucent sheets meet at the origin: the teal xy-plane, the blue-violet xz-plane, and the peach yz-plane. Together they cut all of space into eight wedges called octants.",
    "state": {
      "planeEmphasis": "none"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Octant I",
    "narration": "The near upper wedge, labeled I, is where x, y and z are all positive. Hover any other wedge to read off its sign triple.",
    "state": {
      "planeEmphasis": "none"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Crossing the xy-plane flips z",
    "narration": "With the horizontal teal plane emphasized, everything above it has z > 0 and everything below has z < 0 — only the third sign changes.",
    "state": {
      "planeEmphasis": "xy-plane (z = 0)"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Crossing the xz-plane flips y",
    "narration": "Emphasizing the blue-violet plane y = 0 splits space into y > 0 (screen-right) and y < 0 (screen-left); x and z signs are untouched.",
    "state": {
      "planeEmphasis": "xz-plane (y = 0)"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Crossing the yz-plane flips x",
    "narration": "The peach plane x = 0 separates the wedges in front of it (x > 0, toward the viewer's lower-left) from those behind it (x < 0). Three independent sign choices give 2 x 2 x 2 = 8 octants.",
    "state": {
      "planeEmphasis": "yz-plane (x = 0)"
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "rollDeg": 0,
  "heightFraction": 0.7
}

NOTES: Use an OrthographicCamera in a standard isometric pose for the axonometric look. Render the three planes as PlaneGeometry meshes with MeshBasicMaterial (transparent, depthWrite:false, side:DoubleSide) and sort them into a single render group so overlap tints blend evenly; add LineSegments outlines from EdgesGeometry for the sheet borders. Axes: solid Line + LineDashedMaterial (call computeLineDistances) for the occluded stretches, plus Mesh cones for arrowheads. All text via canvas-texture Sprites so it stays upright and readable while orbiting; keep sprite scale constant in world units. Octant hover uses invisible Box meshes (visible:false is not raycastable, so use material.opacity 0 with transparent:true) intersected by Raycaster; on hover, fade in a thin colored box and swap the label sprite texture to a larger variant. The plane-emphasis toggle raises the chosen sheet's opacity to ~0.8, drops the other two to ~0.15, and tints the two half-spaces with large faint boxes (e.g. warm for the positive side, cool for the negative side) plus a small legend reading 'z > 0 / z < 0'. OrbitControls with damping, no pan, limited zoom; a 'reset view' snap back to the source pose.

Follow the interaction plan above.

